The socio-economic and employment situation in Finland
The forest and metal industry sectors have been the most important part of the Finnish economy.
During the 1990s, the IT industry saw fast growth, rst in mobile phone production and later in other information technology sectors.
Today, the country’s economic growth is rmly based on the development, marketing and export of a wider range of skills.
Education and training, combined with strong investment in research and development (R & D), are therefore crucial to Finland’s continued prosperity.
Since 1995, small and medium-sized enterprises have created more than 75% of the new jobs in Finland.
The challenge for companies now is increasingly to ‘internationalise’and take advantage of the global market place.
Although Finland’s current account surplus has been sound for the past ten years, GNP growth is set to slow to around 2% by 2010 and drop even further after that.
Smaller work input, owing in part to a rapidly ageing workforce, means economic growth will come to depend more and more on increased productivity.
The average working age is set to rise faster proportionally in Finland than in other European countries. By 2025, there will be 50% more 65-year-olds than at present.
Finland must prepare for and properly manage these demographic changes and do so more urgently than its European neighbours.
Encouraging older workers to stay longer in jobs, and persuading young people to start their working careers earlier will be a means of combating an anticipated lack of labour force.
Although this lack is anticipated, the problem of long-term unemployment still exists.
Almost half the long-term jobless are aged over 50.
The broader challenge is to address the high levels of structural unemployment.
Measures to help people in a lessfavourable position in the labour market have so far not been suficiently effective. ective.
As Finnish industrial production has diversified, there is a need to develop secondary schools, especially as regards vocational training.
Finland has set high targets to give at least vocational training to almost all the younger generation.
This high target, however, may lead to a high drop-out rate which should be tackled to prevent the problem of a lack of labour and unemployment occurring at the same time.
Elsewhere, sluggish productivity has been a problem in particular for the service sector, partly due to the lack of competition. So, in addition to a strong competition policy, measures are needed to improve the uptake of ICT technologies across all sectors, as well as public administration, so as to modernise and boost eficiency. ciency.
Finland’s ESF priorities, 2007-2013
These schemes are complemented by measures to help small and medium-sized companies deal with changes in global trade, technology and demographics, thus helping them boost their international growth potential.
The ESF programme for continental Finland applies nationwide, excluding the Åland Islands, and aims to promote competitiveness while working to reduce high levels of structural unemployment.
Specific measures are being introduced or extended to help disadvantaged groups such as the disabled, migrants, and younger and older workers.
The autonomous Åland Islands’separate ESF programme shares many of these goals, while addressing its shortage of qualified employees.
It will achieve the latter by upgrading skill levels among the local workforce to better meet the challenge of globalisation and helping to attract and better integrate foreign workers.
To this end, Åland’s programme provides for counselling, training, work placements and job subsidies, while giving precedence to gender equality projects.
In this respect, vocational training is a key development area, as are measures designed not only to help people back into work and training but also to help them stay there.
Supporting equal opportunities by fighting discrimination in education, training and access to jobs.
Priority 1: Developing work organisations, the employed labour force and companies, and encouraging entrepreneurship
Priority 3: Development of know-how, innovation and services systems that promote the functioning of the labour market
Helping companies, especially SMEs, to manage changes due to globalisation, technological development and an ageing workforce
Reinforcing the growth potential of SMEs in a sustainable way and with an international perspective
Matching education and training with the realities of working life: improving access to vocational training, especially for women
Supporting new entrepreneurship schemes
Giving job-seekers practical help for entering the workplace via better information and guidance.
Promoting gender equality in the workplace.
Priority 4: Transnational and inter-regional ESF activities
Priority 2: Promoting access to employment and sustainable inclusion in the labour market, and preventing social exclusion
Improving co-operation in employment, education and industrial policy by promoting labour immigration
Decreasing structural unemployment by training young people in key areas and promoting lifelong learning among those already in the labour market
Generating official and unofficial networks and developing the skills of experts.
Helping to ensure youngsters achieve academic goals, while reducing the numbers dropping out of education
